  "nameStory": "The 'clustered bonnet' gets its name from its habit of growing in tight groups or clusters, like a bunch of tiny caps huddled together on old wood. The 'bonnet' part refers to the bell-shaped cap that resembles a small, neat hat. It's also called the 'oak-stump bonnet' because it has a particular fondness for growing on oak stumps and logs.",
  "description": "The clustered bonnet is a small, delicate mushroom with a reddish-brown bell-shaped cap that looks like a tiny umbrella. These mushrooms grow in clusters or small groups on decaying wood, especially oak stumps and fallen logs. The caps reach up to about 1.8 inches across, while the slender stems can stretch up to 3.5 inches tall. What makes them distinctive is their tendency to grow together in tight bunches, creating charming little mushroom communities on rotting wood.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Growth Pattern",
      "description": "Always grows in clusters or small groups on decaying wood, never alone - look for multiple mushrooms sprouting from the same spot on logs or stumps",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Bell-shaped and reddish-brown, up to 1.8 inches across, feels smooth to the touch and maintains its rounded shape even when mature",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Thin and delicate, whitish at the top but becoming reddish-brown toward the base, often covered with yellowish fuzzy material (mycelium) at the bottom third",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Pale brown to pinkish in color, attached to the stem, and relatively widely spaced for such a small mushroom",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Pure white when collected on paper - place cap gill-side down overnight to see this diagnostic feature",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Safety Note",
      "description": "Considered doubtfully edible - never eat any wild mushroom without expert identification, as many small brown mushrooms can be dangerous",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for clustered bonnets on decaying hardwood logs and stumps throughout New England's forests, with a special preference for oak wood. They thrive in moist, shaded areas where fallen trees and old stumps provide the perfect rotting wood substrate. You'll find them in both deciduous and mixed forests, from lowland woods to mid-elevation slopes.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Early clusters may begin appearing on logs as temperatures warm and spring rains provide moisture. Look for small, fresh clusters with bright colors.",
    "summer": "Peak fruiting season when moisture levels are right - clusters are most abundant after summer rain showers on humid days.",
    "fall": "Continue fruiting through autumn, especially during wet periods. Fall specimens may appear more weathered but are still identifiable by their clustering habit.",
    "winter": "Rarely visible during cold months, though the mycelium continues working inside the wood, preparing for next season's mushrooms."
  },
  "funFact": "The yellowish fuzzy material at the base of clustered bonnet stems is actually the mushroom's 'root system' (mycelium) that's working like a tiny recycling factory, breaking down dead oak wood and turning it back into soil nutrients that feed the forest.",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with the common bonnet (Mycena galericulata), but clustered bonnets have that distinctive reddish-brown color and always grow in tight groups, while common bonnets are grayer and often grow more scattered. Remember: 'clustered and colorful' versus 'common and gray.'",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The Mycena genus includes over 30 species in New England, making it one of the most diverse groups of small mushrooms in our region. Many Mycenas look quite similar, so paying attention to substrate (what they're growing on), growth pattern, and color details becomes crucial for accurate identification.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike its relatives the common bonnet and bleeding fairy helmet, clustered bonnet stands out with its reddish-brown coloration and obligate clustering behavior. The bleeding fairy helmet 'bleeds' red liquid when cut, while the common bonnet is typically grayish and grows more individually. Clustered bonnet also shows a stronger preference for oak wood than most of its cousins.",
